  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Design and delivery of employment and skills policy improved at central and local levels</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Despite progress in employment, young people, particularly women, face high unemployment and inactivity. To address this, Albania is gradually establishing a Youth Guarantee (YG), ensuring that all young people under 30 who are not in employment, education, or training (NEETs) receive quality offers of jobs, traineeships, apprenticeships, or further education within four months. Supported by the EU-ILO Technical Assistance Facility (TAF), the ILO provides policy guidance, strengthens service delivery, and improves outreach to maximize the impact of YG implementation. 

Furthermore, Albania faces challenges in aligning skills development training with labour market needs and upskilling underserved populations, in particular in rural areas. The ILO works with qualification and training authorities, Sectoral Skills Councils and training providers, to enhance their capacities in skills anticipation and training delivery. Through a joint UN project on promoting digital technologies in agriculture, the ILO supports sectoral skills councils, the updating curricula of Technical Vocational Education and Training (TVET), and developing training for smallholders, particularly women and youth.  The ILO works with all TVET providers with agricultural profiles, the Tirana Agricultural University, agricultural extension services and other relevant partners close to farmers.  

Through the UNJP on Global Accelerator, the ILO will skill, reskill and upskill the laid off women from TFCL sector, registered as job seekers at NAES, and will facilitate their transition to tourism sector, already suffering from lack of qualified workforce.</narrative>
  </description>
